<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  This manifest exists for exactly one reason: without it the isolation tests
  cannot load their own fixtures, and BIT-62 is the first time anyone found out,
  because BIT-58's tests had only ever been COMPILED.

  ThirdPartyIsolationTest and CrossOriginIframeIsolationTest serve their hostile
  pages from LocalTestServer, which is a plain HTTP server on 127.0.0.1 — it has
  to be, because the point is to control the origin, and a self-signed https
  origin would trip SSL handling rather than the thing under test.

  Cleartext HTTP has been refused by default since targetSdk 28, and there is no
  loopback exception in the platform's default network security config. This
  module's test APK targets far past that. So `loadUrl("http://127.0.0.1:41001/…")`
  fails with net::ERR_CLEARTEXT_NOT_PERMITTED, the WebView commits an error page,
  onProgressChanged still reaches 100 — so the CountDownLatch in loadHostilePage()
  is released and the test does NOT hang — and the probe object the assertions read
  back was never created.

  Note what that produces, because it is the whole argument for the canary test:
  three of the nine tests go red (the two that read a probe, and
  theCrossOriginIframeActuallyRan), and the rest go GREEN having proved nothing.
  "No LNURL endpoint was contacted" and "the page never loaded" are the same tick.
  theCrossOriginIframeActuallyRan is what turns that into a red, exactly as BIT-58
  intended when it insisted on keeping it as a separate test.

  WHY A DOMAIN-SCOPED CONFIG AND NOT usesCleartextTraffic="true"

  The one-attribute version would re-permit cleartext to everything for the test
  APK, so a future test that quietly depended on plaintext traffic to a real host
  would work instead of failing. The config below permits 127.0.0.1 and localhost
  and nothing else.

  WHY THIS CANNOT REACH THE SHIPPED APP

  It is in src/androidTest/, which is only ever built into the instrumentation
  test APK. It is not in src/main/ and not in src/debug/, so it is absent from
  :app's merged manifest in every build type. The R-11 baseline that
  theHardeningBaselineIsAppliedToTheRealWebView asserts is untouched: this changes
  what the operating system will route, not one setting on the WebView.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <application android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config_androidtest" />

</manifest>
